title: 'The Cepheid Bias: Resolving the Hubble Tension'
message: If you use this software, please cite it as below.
type: article
authors:
- family-names: Smawfield
  given-names: Matthew Lukin
  orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0003-8219-3159
abstract: >-
  The persistent 5-sigma discrepancy between the local distance ladder (H0 ~ 73.0 km/s/Mpc) and early-universe cosmological inference under flat LambdaCDM (H0 = 67.4 +/- 0.5 km/s/Mpc) represents a fundamental challenge in precision cosmology. This work demonstrates that this tension arises from an environment-dependent clock bias predicted by the Temporal Equivalence Principle (TEP), wherein proper time is a dynamical scalar field that couples universally to non-gravitational matter and clocks. Because stellar pulsation rates operate in local proper time, calibrating classical Cepheids in deep-potential SN Ia host galaxies against diffuse, low-mass anchor galaxies systematically misestimates host distance moduli.

  This framework is tested using the complete public Riess et al. (2022; R22) sample of 37 distinct SN Ia host galaxies matched by exact supernova and PGC identifiers, with a homogeneous kinematic potential coordinate u_phi = V_rot / sqrt(2) derived from pinned HyperLEDA rotation velocities, RC3 diameters, and continuous environmental screening. Single-galaxy differential tests provide internal signatures with the sign predicted by TEP free from host-to-host peculiar velocity systematics: in M31, inner versus outer Cepheids exhibit an empirical Period-Luminosity offset of Delta W = W_inner - W_outer = +0.356 +/- 0.136 mag (2.6 sigma), increasing to +0.630 +/- 0.195 mag (3.24 sigma) under spatial PHAT matching, while OGLE-IV Cepheids in the LMC independently exhibit radial potential stratification at +0.0284 +/- 0.0086 mag (3.3 sigma).

  Across all 37 hosts, the generative endpoint likelihood yields Gamma_X = (1.156 +/- 0.958) x 10^7 km/s/Mpc under the canonical 250 km/s velocity variance, strengthening to 1.64 sigma under data-driven Pantheon+ velocity scatter (182.1 km/s) and 1.97 sigma at 150 km/s, with 100% positive leave-one-host-out stability (37 of 37 refits positive). In the cosmologically constrained host-level expansion-rate likelihood across the 33 Hubble-flow hosts (Step 42), the combined endpoint response is recovered at Gamma_X = (1.165 +/- 0.979) x 10^7 km/s/Mpc (1.19 sigma; 2.07 sigma at 150 km/s; and 1.99 sigma under full 33x33 SH0ES covariance GLS), yielding a conventional Hubble-flow intercept of H_app = 68.36 +/- 0.98 km/s/Mpc to 68.62 +/- 1.51 km/s/Mpc. When evaluated in a joint multi-block framework with independent TRGB distances and geometric anchors (Step 44), the multi-block likelihood consistently favours an environmental response under either single-parameter allocation (kappa_Cep = (0.400 +/- 0.270) x 10^6 mag or beta_X = (1.631 +/- 1.250) x 10^7 km/s/Mpc), resolving the local Hubble constant to H_app = 68.76 +/- 1.02 km/s/Mpc. Under the restricted TEP Cepheid-channel closure (beta_X = 0), the full response is allocated to the Cepheid channel (kappa_Cep_equiv = (0.369 +/- 0.310) x 10^6 mag); this allocation is physically specified but remains conditional on host-specific aperture validation.

  Propagating the TEP potential correction through the distance ladder yields a unified local Hubble constant of H0 = 66.65 +/- 1.58 km/s/Mpc, consistent with Planck CMB observations at 0.45 sigma (0.31 sigma bootstrap). Full-matrix propagation through the 3,490-row SH0ES system confirms exact reference-gauge invariance. These multi-scale empirical findings indicate that the Hubble tension can be resolved by dynamical proper time in the local distance scale, pending host-specific aperture validation of the Cepheid-channel allocation.
